#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_hankel_transform

#% text
# `inverse_hankel_transform(g, s, r)` gives the inverse Hankel transform of order 0, `integrate(g bessel_j(0, s r) s, [s, 0, inf])`; `inverse_hankel_transform(g, s, r, nu)` of order nu.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The Hankel transform is its own inverse, so the forms are those of `hankel_transform`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_hankel_transform(exp(-s)/s, s, r)
#% output
#= (1 + r^2)^(-1/2)

#% cell
inverse_hankel_transform(1/(1 + s^2)^(3/2), s, r)
#% output
#= exp(-r)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `hankel_transform`
